{"name":"Sunflower Island","hex":"#ffcd01","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sunflower","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Helianthus, so called probably from the form and color of its floral head, having the form of a large disk surrounded by yellow ray flowers.\n\nThe commonly cultivated species, Helianthus annuus, a native of America.","The commonly cultivated species, Helianthus annuus, a native of America."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunflower"},{"term":"island","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, totally surrounded by water.","A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, partially surrounded by water; a peninsula; a half-island."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround with water; make into an island.","To set, dot (as if) with islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/island"}],"entries":[{"title":"Common sunflower","blocks":[{"text":"The common sunflower is a large annual forb in the daisy family Asteraceae. The domesticated form of common sunflower is harvested for its edible seeds, which come in two types: oil and confectionery seeds. Oilseed sunflowers are widely grown globally and represent the fourth most used vegetable oil in the world. They also are used widely as bird food or as food for livestock. In contrast, confectionery sunflower seeds are often eaten as a snack food or in baking. There also are horticultural sunflower varieties that are used as plantings in domestic gardens for aesthetics. Wild plants are known for their multiple flower heads, whereas the domestic sunflower often possesses a single large flower head atop an unbranched stem."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg/330px-Sunflower_sky_backdrop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_sunflower","wikidata":"Q171497"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island","wikidata":"Q23442"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/1280px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SunflowerSunset2.jpg/500px-SunflowerSunset2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Flower heads facing east, away from the late afternoon sun","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASunflowerSunset2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sunflower_macro_wide.jpg/500px-Sunflower_macro_wide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sunflower_macro_wide.jpg/960px-Sunflower_macro_wide.jpg?utm_source=en.wi